## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design algorithms with provable performance guarantees, handle pathological inputs gracefully, and understand trade-offs between different sorting approaches. It's particularly relevant for large-scale systems where worst-case behavior impacts reliability.

## Key Concepts
- **Introsort**: Hybrid algorithm combining quicksort's average-case efficiency with heapsort's worst-case guarantees
- **3-way partitioning**: Dutch national flag algorithm that efficiently handles duplicate keys
- **Depth monitoring**: Switches to heapsort when recursion depth exceeds 2⌊log₂(n)⌋ to maintain O(n log n) worst-case
- **Space complexity**: O(log n) stack space through tail-call optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n || 1));

  function sort(left, right, depth) {
    if (left >= right) return;

    if (depth === 0) {
      heapSort(arr, left, right);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = threeWayPartition(arr, left, right);

    // Sort smaller partition first (tail recursion optimization)
    if (lt - left < right - gt) {
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
    } else {
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
    }
  }

  sort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine basic structures (arrays/deque, hashmap, heap) to maintain a sliding window’s frequency distribution and extract top-K on demand.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance with a deque
- Frequency counting via a hashmap
- Min-heap with lazy deletions for top-K
- Amortized update and top-K retrieval costs

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W
    this.window = [] // acts as deque (push is at end, shift from start)
    this.counts = new Map()
    this.heap = [] // min-heap of [freq, value]
  }
  _pushHeap(pair) { /* heap push for [freq, value] */ }
  add(x) {
    this.window.push(x)
    this.counts.set(x, (this.counts.get(x) || 0) + 1)
    // push updated freq
    this._pushHeap([this.counts.get(x), x])
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      const y = this.window.shift()
      const f = (this.counts.get(y) || 0) - 1
      if (f <= 0) this.counts.delete(y)
      else this.counts.set(y, f)
      this._pushHeap([f, y])
    }
  }
  topK(K) {
    const res = []
    const seen = new Set()
    const pulled = []
    while (res.length < Math.min(K, this.counts.size) && this.heap.length) {
      const [freq, val] = this._popHeap()
      if (!this.counts.has(val) || this.counts.get(val) !== freq) continue
      if (!seen.has(val)) {
        seen.add(val)
        res.push(val)
        pulled.push([freq, val])
      }
    }
    // restore elements for future calls
    for (const p of pulled) this._pushHeap(p)
    return res
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of multiple shortest paths and second-best paths with counting, using a single Dijkstra variant. It blends graph traversal and path combinatorics under constraints typical at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Two-best paths per node; counting paths for each distance tier
- Dijkstra relaxation with tie-handling
- Modulo counting for combinatorial paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Two-best Dijkstra (conceptual)
function secondShortest(graph, s, t, MOD){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist1 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const dist2 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const cnt1 = Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt2 = Array(n).fill(0);
  dist1[s] = 0; cnt1[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap();
  pq.push([0, s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist2[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of graph[u]){
      const nd = d + w;
      if (nd < dist1[v]){
        dist2[v] = dist1[v]; cnt2[v] = cnt1[v];
        dist1[v] = nd; cnt1[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist1[v]){
        cnt1[v] = (cnt1[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      } else if (nd > dist1[v] && nd < dist2[v]){
        dist2[v] = nd; cnt2[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist2[v]){
        cnt2[v] = (cnt2[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return [dist2[t], cnt2[t] % MOD];
}
```

